  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Harinadha Nunna, vs The State Of A.P. on 15 October, 2020 The Hon'Ble Smt Justice Lalitha Kanneganti Criminal Petition No.4500 of 2020 Order:",
    "facts": "The petitioners before this Court are accused Nos.1 to 8 in the above said crime. 3. The brief facts of the case are that above crime was registered against the petitioners on the ground of transporting and storing of tobacco and gutkha products which are banned by the Government.",
    "arguments": "Learned counsel for the petitioner submits that Sections 270 and 273 I.P.C relate to food safety standards. Section 270 I.P.C speaks about malignant act likely to spread infection of disease dangerous to life and Section 273 of I.P.C speaks about sale of Noxious Food or Drink. None of the sections under which the present crime is registered, is applicable to the present case as tobacco and gutkha will not spread any infection and it does not fall under category of food or drink or poison. Therefore, the police have no power to register the offences under above sections except by the designated officers like Food Safety Officer. 6. Learned counsel for the petitioners submits that this Court in similar circumstances passed common order dated 18.12.2019 in Criminal Petition Nos.5421 of 2019 and batch, wherein it was held that tobacco does not fall within the definition of \"food\" as specified under Section 3(i)(j) of the FSS Act and so, registration of FIR is not permissible.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "Learned counsel for the petitioner submits that Sections 270 and 273 I.P.C relate to food safety standards. Section 270 I.P.C speaks about malignant act likely to spread infection of disease dangerous to life and Section 273 of I.P.C speaks about sale of Noxious Food or Drink. None of the sections under which the present crime is registered, is applicable to the present case as tobacco and gutkha will not spread any infection and it does not fall under category of food or drink or poison. Therefore, the police have no power to register the offences under above sections except by the designated officers like Food Safety Officer. 6. Learned counsel for the petitioners submits that this Court in similar circumstances passed common order dated 18.12.2019 in Criminal Petition Nos.5421 of 2019 and batch, wherein it was held that tobacco does not fall within the definition of \"food\" as specified under Section 3(i)(j) of the FSS Act and so, registration of FIR is not permissible.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
